## Assembling the pieces

You've now met every ingredient of a
<GlossaryPopover term="transformer">transformer</GlossaryPopover>: token vectors (Ch 5), layers of
neurons (Ch 6), and attention (Ch 9). A transformer arranges them into a **block** and stacks that
block many times.<Cite id="vaswani2017" /> Build GPT-2 small one piece at a time:

<BlockBuilder />

Two pieces are new:

- **The "+" circles.** Attention and the neuron layer (called the **MLP**) don't replace a token's
  vector; they add a change to it. The original passes straight through on a highway, and each
  block adds its contribution. This trick, called a residual connection, makes very deep networks
  much easier to train.<Cite id="he2016" />
- **Layer norm.** Before each step, the numbers are rescaled to a steady size, which also helps
  training stay stable.<Cite id="ba2016" /> GPT-2 puts it at the start of each step, plus once more
  at the very end.<Cite id="radford2019" />

The counter shows where GPT-2's 124 million weights live. About a third are in the token and
position tables at the start. Almost half are in the MLPs, which take up two thirds of every block.
Attention, the star of the last chapter, has fewer than a quarter.

## Watch a guess take shape

That highway is called the
<GlossaryPopover term="residual-stream">residual stream</GlossaryPopover>. Every block reads from
it and writes back to it, and the final stream becomes the prediction. So what if you stopped early
and asked for a prediction from the middle of the stack? Researchers do exactly that, a trick
called the logit lens.<Cite id="nostalgebraist2020" /> Here it is on "The cat sat on the":

<ResidualStream />

At the start, the stream is just the vectors for " the" and its position, and read as a guess it's
nonsense. From block 1 to block 7 the top guess is " same", as in "sat on the same": a sensible kind
of word, but not about cats. From block 8 on, " floor" is at or near the top (" ground" edges ahead
once), and furniture appears: " table", " sofa", " bed". The last block spreads the bets out again,
ending at " floor", " bed", and " couch", the same guesses you saw in Ch 0.

No single block "knows" the answer. Each one nudges the stream, and the prediction sharpens as it
goes.

<RecapCard>

- A transformer block is attention plus an MLP, each added onto the residual stream, with layer
  norm before each.
- GPT-2 small stacks 12 blocks. Most of its weights are in the MLPs and the token table, not in
  attention.
- Each block makes a small change; reading the stream partway through shows the guess forming.

</RecapCard>

**Next question:** the model gives probabilities for one next token. How does that become a whole
paragraph?
